00:54:36.743 --> 00:54:37.920
- They can fit through anything.

00:54:37.920 --> 00:54:39.630
So they would go under the door,

00:54:39.630 --> 00:54:41.490
even though the clearance is like this,

00:54:41.490 --> 00:54:43.382
they would like squeeze underneath.

00:54:43.382 --> 00:54:45.120
- They turn into a liquid form.

00:54:45.120 --> 00:54:46.440
- Literally, literally dude.

00:54:46.440 --> 00:54:48.007
I remember 'cause we were...

00:54:48.007 --> 00:54:51.720
it was so funny 'cause
this maisonette in London

00:54:51.720 --> 00:54:53.940
that was created, it was being touted

00:54:53.940 --> 00:54:55.470
as being rat and mouse proof.

00:54:55.470 --> 00:54:56.735
That was the whole point
behind the building.

00:54:56.735 --> 00:54:57.568
- Oh yeah.
- Yeah.

00:54:57.568 --> 00:54:59.700
- And the landlord was extremely furious

00:54:59.700 --> 00:55:01.170
when I told him about it.

00:55:01.170 --> 00:55:03.090
I was like, "Yeah man, oh, we got mice."

00:55:03.090 --> 00:55:04.950
And he's like, "What?

00:55:04.950 --> 00:55:05.987
I bought this place specifically

00:55:05.987 --> 00:55:08.100
'cause I was told that
it couldn't have mice."

00:55:08.100 --> 00:55:09.820
I'm like, "Well we got mice". And so he-

00:55:09.820 --> 00:55:11.329
- How do you make a
place mouse and rat free?

00:55:11.329 --> 00:55:14.483
- It's not possible. I
don't think it's possible.

00:55:14.483 --> 00:55:17.160
And so we had these mice

00:55:17.160 --> 00:55:19.853
and they always came out at night.

00:55:19.853 --> 00:55:21.750
I dunno if that's normal.
- Yeah.

00:55:21.750 --> 00:55:23.187
- I never saw them during the day, but-

00:55:23.187 --> 00:55:24.659
- Are they nocturnal?

00:55:24.659 --> 00:55:25.680
- I dunno, it was horrible though

00:55:25.680 --> 00:55:27.960
'cause I'd be in my bed watching Netflix.

00:55:27.960 --> 00:55:31.092
TV's there, door is here
and I'd just see a shadow.

00:55:31.092 --> 00:55:31.925
(imitates air whooshing)

00:55:31.925 --> 00:55:32.924
- Oh shit.

00:55:32.924 --> 00:55:33.960
- In my room.

00:55:33.960 --> 00:55:35.010
- Yeah, I've had that before.

00:55:35.010 --> 00:55:36.107
- And I was like, what the fuck?

00:55:36.107 --> 00:55:38.880
- That's so weird, we don't
get mice and rats in Australia.

00:55:38.880 --> 00:55:39.810
- Might be too hot.
- I don't know.

00:55:39.810 --> 00:55:40.920
- Well I think it's 'cause all the snakes

00:55:40.920 --> 00:55:41.753
are fucking eating them.

00:55:41.753 --> 00:55:42.990
- Yeah.
- Right.

00:55:42.990 --> 00:55:44.320
- They get like fucked by
snakes and stuff like that.

00:55:44.320 --> 00:55:45.545
- Yeah, oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:55:45.545 --> 00:55:47.295
- And cats and stuff.

00:55:48.150 --> 00:55:51.180
We tried all this expensive
stuff for killing them.

00:55:51.180 --> 00:55:54.600
In the end, the thing that
killed them the most was just-

00:55:54.600 --> 00:55:55.650
- Mouse trap?

00:55:55.650 --> 00:55:57.690
- The basic Amazon mouse trap.

00:55:57.690 --> 00:55:58.860
It would clap their cheese.

00:55:58.860 --> 00:56:00.540
- Don't fix something
that's not broken, right?

00:56:00.540 --> 00:56:03.750
- I think the poison killed some of them,

00:56:03.750 --> 00:56:05.670
but we ended up just
needing to like clog it.

00:56:05.670 --> 00:56:07.570
But the thing is, they
chew through everything.

00:56:07.570 --> 00:56:08.460
- Yeah.
- Yeah yeah, yeah.

00:56:08.460 --> 00:56:10.129
- No matter what you
clog it with, they just,

00:56:10.129 --> 00:56:12.570
they get through it.

00:56:12.570 --> 00:56:15.930
Like in New York, the rats
can chew through concrete.

00:56:15.930 --> 00:56:16.890
- Yeah, yeah. Yeah.
- Holy shit.

00:56:16.890 --> 00:56:18.060
- Little by little, right?

00:56:18.060 --> 00:56:19.770
- Insane, it's insane.

00:56:19.770 --> 00:56:21.750
- The one pest that-
- That's some Shawshank shit.

00:56:21.750 --> 00:56:23.280
- Yeah, the one pest that terrifies me,

00:56:23.280 --> 00:56:24.840
of ever getting, is termites.

00:56:24.840 --> 00:56:26.135
I've never known anyone with termites.

00:56:26.135 --> 00:56:27.967
- Oh, we had termites.

00:56:27.967 --> 00:56:29.256
- It's not really a problem
unless you have a wood house,

00:56:29.256 --> 00:56:30.089
though, isn't it?

00:56:30.089 --> 00:56:31.890
- Yeah yeah, yeah, 'cause
at my parents' place

00:56:31.890 --> 00:56:34.290
had a termite infestation and it was scary

00:56:34.290 --> 00:56:36.690
because it's like, oh the
house might come down.

00:56:37.530 --> 00:56:39.313
- It's the one of the few pests that,

00:56:39.313 --> 00:56:40.230
if you have it in your house,

00:56:40.230 --> 00:56:42.777
and under the right conditions,
it could just kill you.

00:56:42.777 --> 00:56:43.650
Right, because you-

00:56:43.650 --> 00:56:45.750
- Yeah, 'cause wooden houses
in Australia mainly, right?

00:56:45.750 --> 00:56:46.643
- Yes.
- Yeah.

00:56:46.643 --> 00:56:47.520
- So yeah.

00:56:47.520 --> 00:56:48.630
- How'd you deal with termites,

00:56:48.630 --> 00:56:50.223
like what's the process around
getting rid of termites?

00:56:50.223 --> 00:56:51.510
- When it comes to termites,

00:56:51.510 --> 00:56:53.460
you just have to call
a specialist for that.

00:56:53.460 --> 00:56:54.930
- Don't they just like gas the place?

00:56:54.930 --> 00:56:57.060
- Pretty much. They like
smoke it out basically.

00:56:57.060 --> 00:56:58.170
- Right?

00:56:58.170 --> 00:57:00.060
- Yeah, we had a termite,

00:57:00.060 --> 00:57:02.490
it wasn't a massive
infestation, but we were like,

00:57:02.490 --> 00:57:04.350
yeah, we should probably
get rid of these now

00:57:04.350 --> 00:57:07.710
because if they expand, yeah,
the house is gonna come down.

00:57:07.710 --> 00:57:09.690
- Yeah.
- Like, so fuck that.

00:57:09.690 --> 00:57:10.800
- That's fucking terrifying.

00:57:10.800 --> 00:57:11.633
- It's so scary.

00:57:11.633 --> 00:57:13.230
- That's so sick that when they do that,

00:57:13.230 --> 00:57:15.780
like when they just put
gas all in the house.

00:57:15.780 --> 00:57:17.040
- Yeah.
- I find that so cool.

00:57:17.040 --> 00:57:18.598
- Well, why?
- Why is that cool?

00:57:18.598 --> 00:57:19.770
- It's like, wow, they
just put like a tent

00:57:19.770 --> 00:57:20.820
over the house and gas it.

00:57:20.820 --> 00:57:22.227
I'm like, that's so badass.

00:57:22.227 --> 00:57:24.870
Who thought, "Oh, we
gotta kill these rats.

00:57:24.870 --> 00:57:27.480
Oh, Oh we gotta use these rat poison."

00:57:27.480 --> 00:57:29.310
And the guy's like, "Hold up.

00:57:29.310 --> 00:57:30.915
Boys, what if we gas the whole house."

00:57:30.915 --> 00:57:31.860
- What if we nuke the house?

00:57:31.860 --> 00:57:35.190
- And I'm like, the first
time someone suggested that

00:57:35.190 --> 00:57:36.720
someone must been like, you're crazy.

00:57:36.720 --> 00:57:38.340
Like, no, no, hold on.

00:57:38.340 --> 00:57:39.660
What if we put a big tent,

00:57:39.660 --> 00:57:42.270
a comically large tent around the house.

00:57:42.270 --> 00:57:43.980
Someone's like, you're a genius.

00:57:43.980 --> 00:57:45.180
Let's try it.

00:57:45.180 --> 00:57:47.370
- How else are they gonna
get rid of thousands

00:57:47.370 --> 00:57:50.223
of tiny insects just living
in every single crevice.

00:57:50.223 --> 00:57:53.156
- They're not gonna be like...

00:57:53.156 --> 00:57:53.989
- You're gonna be like,

00:57:53.989 --> 00:57:57.210
well it's either like the
tent or the flame thrower.

00:57:57.210 --> 00:57:59.730
And I'm like, before we
get the flame thrower out,

00:57:59.730 --> 00:58:02.220
maybe we should throw out a tent, maybe.

00:58:02.220 --> 00:58:04.110
- I think the one job on earth,

00:58:04.110 --> 00:58:05.730
sort of somewhat related to this,

00:58:05.730 --> 00:58:08.880
not dealing with getting
rid of bugs and stuff, but.

00:58:08.880 --> 00:58:10.012
You know people who clean houses

00:58:10.012 --> 00:58:11.970
after people have died.
- Yeah.

00:58:11.970 --> 00:58:14.700
That's the one job I
don't think I could do.

00:58:14.700 --> 00:58:16.314
- Oh, it depends how horribly they die.

00:58:16.314 --> 00:58:17.147
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:58:17.147 --> 00:58:18.900
- Well, 'cause normally, you
know, when you get older,

00:58:18.900 --> 00:58:20.160
you struggle to take care of yourself,

00:58:20.160 --> 00:58:23.550
so the house gets really
like, you know, fucked.

00:58:23.550 --> 00:58:26.987
And I saw one on Japan, so
depressing and it's just like-

00:58:26.987 --> 00:58:28.083
- No, it happens very often in Japan.

00:58:28.083 --> 00:58:29.400
- Yeah yeah, yeah.

00:58:29.400 --> 00:58:30.810
And they go in and they're just like,

00:58:30.810 --> 00:58:32.264
well, we're just gonna take
all this guy's possessions

00:58:32.264 --> 00:58:34.170
and trash them and stuff.

00:58:34.170 --> 00:58:36.270
It's like, "Oh, oh, don't
break my heart, dude.

00:58:36.270 --> 00:58:37.830
Just go into someone's
house trying to figure out,

00:58:37.830 --> 00:58:40.500
what their life was and
stuff, I couldn't do it.

00:58:40.500 --> 00:58:42.270
- Oh, I mean, I guess, I mean like.

00:58:42.270 --> 00:58:44.010
- You're like, oh, I don't give a fuck.

00:58:44.010 --> 00:58:45.260
Bring in the trash truck.

00:58:46.260 --> 00:58:47.093
- I thought you meant people

00:58:47.093 --> 00:58:48.563
who had been brutally
murdered or something.

00:58:48.563 --> 00:58:49.949
- Yeah, that's what I thought.

00:58:49.949 --> 00:58:51.510
- I was like, yeah,
that's a pretty bad job.

00:58:51.510 --> 00:58:52.800
- I mean, that's probably one part of it,

00:58:52.800 --> 00:58:56.550
but what if you're a guy who
couldn't take care of yourself,

00:58:56.550 --> 00:58:58.470
you're like 70 and you know,

00:58:58.470 --> 00:59:01.323
maybe you couldn't make it
to the toilet and stuff.

00:59:01.323 --> 00:59:02.453
- I don't know, I feel like-

00:59:02.453 --> 00:59:04.440
- And the room smells-
- Like you shit yourself.

00:59:04.440 --> 00:59:06.870
- Well yeah, it happens, it happens.

00:59:06.870 --> 00:59:07.950
- Yeah, of course.

00:59:07.950 --> 00:59:09.960
I mean, I think that'd be like a fine job.

00:59:09.960 --> 00:59:10.793
I mean, it's-

00:59:10.793 --> 00:59:12.090
- What? Can't say I'll do it.

00:59:12.090 --> 00:59:13.140
Gimme a tenner.

00:59:13.140 --> 00:59:14.550
- I mean, it's not a pleasant job,

00:59:14.550 --> 00:59:15.540
but I feel like it's...

00:59:15.540 --> 00:59:16.440
- Would it not freak you out,

00:59:16.440 --> 00:59:18.540
just knowing that someone was there,

00:59:18.540 --> 00:59:20.850
and living there, like a week ago.

00:59:20.850 --> 00:59:21.690
That'll freak me out.

00:59:21.690 --> 00:59:23.430
- I think you'd get pretty numb
to it after a while, though.

00:59:23.430 --> 00:59:24.347
- Dunno.
- I mean,

00:59:24.347 --> 00:59:27.360
I feel like cleaning
out all of their stuff,

00:59:27.360 --> 00:59:30.270
you'd just be like, "Oh,
this is someone's legacy.

00:59:30.270 --> 00:59:32.100
This is someone's entire life".

00:59:32.100 --> 00:59:32.933
Why would that freak you out?

00:59:32.933 --> 00:59:35.774
- I dunno, just trying to
figure out what happened?

00:59:35.774 --> 00:59:36.826
Like, what was their life like?

00:59:36.826 --> 00:59:39.025
- Maybe that wouldn't, I think
that's super interesting.

00:59:39.025 --> 00:59:39.930
- [Joey] Yeah.

00:59:39.930 --> 00:59:41.880
- You get a peak into someone's...

00:59:41.880 --> 00:59:44.700
not saying this is like a
glorious job or something,

00:59:44.700 --> 00:59:48.060
but I think, if someone died peacefully

00:59:48.060 --> 00:59:50.520
of old age or something and
you're cleaning their house,

00:59:50.520 --> 00:59:53.490
I think that would be a
really interesting way

00:59:53.490 --> 00:59:57.665
to really piece together
what kind of person they are-

00:59:57.665 --> 00:59:59.940
- I'm think, unfortunately,
in the modern era,

00:59:59.940 --> 01:00:01.380
we have this peaceful ignorance

01:00:01.380 --> 01:00:04.770
that we think that a lot of
deaths are this pleasant.

01:00:04.770 --> 01:00:07.140
He passed away with
his friends and family.

01:00:07.140 --> 01:00:08.190
Especially in Japan and stuff.

01:00:08.190 --> 01:00:11.460
It's like, "No, Tanaka San
died age 90 in his apartment

01:00:11.460 --> 01:00:13.080
and his family hadn't
seen him in five years

01:00:13.080 --> 01:00:15.354
and he doesn't have any friends.

01:00:15.354 --> 01:00:16.516
- And he's just a skeleton now.

01:00:16.516 --> 01:00:18.167
- And it's yeah-
- It happens.

01:00:18.167 --> 01:00:21.360
- 'Cause the video was following
this guy, that I watched,

01:00:21.360 --> 01:00:24.007
and he was cleaning it
out and he was like,

01:00:24.007 --> 01:00:26.250
"Yep, it looks like he
tried to make some food

01:00:26.250 --> 01:00:28.218
right before he passed,
but he wasn't able to."

01:00:28.218 --> 01:00:30.912
And it's like, "Oh".
- Heartbreaking, yeah.

01:00:30.912 --> 01:00:32.700
- Man just wanted a curry, dude.

01:00:32.700 --> 01:00:34.620
We all deserve that, we all
deserve just a nice meal.

01:00:34.620 --> 01:00:35.490
- Before the curry.

01:00:35.490 --> 01:00:37.470
- Wait, so how did they die then?

01:00:37.470 --> 01:00:39.420
- Well, you know, if you
are getting really old

01:00:39.420 --> 01:00:43.170
and you're not able to make
it to the toilet and stuff

01:00:43.170 --> 01:00:44.460
and you can barely get outta bed

01:00:44.460 --> 01:00:45.901
and no one's there to help you.

01:00:45.901 --> 01:00:47.280
You know, it can be really fucked.

01:00:47.280 --> 01:00:48.113
- Oh, yeah, yeah.

01:00:48.113 --> 01:00:50.017
- You know, one day your
heart could just be like,

01:00:50.017 --> 01:00:51.934
"I don't think so", and
then just stop, you know?

01:00:51.934 --> 01:00:52.854
- Wow. Yeah.

01:00:52.854 --> 01:00:54.360
- I mean, I think there are worse ways

01:00:54.360 --> 01:00:55.510
to pass away than that.

01:00:56.675 --> 01:00:58.643
They died in their home and you know,

01:00:58.643 --> 01:01:00.420
I think that would be something

01:01:00.420 --> 01:01:03.360
where a lot of people wish to pass away.

01:01:03.360 --> 01:01:05.400
- I think a worst job is the people

01:01:05.400 --> 01:01:07.770
who have to clean up
after train accidents.

01:01:07.770 --> 01:01:10.664
- Yeah, that would be traumatic for you.

01:01:10.664 --> 01:01:11.497
- That would suck ass.

01:01:11.497 --> 01:01:14.490
- Like to me, if I knew
a guy died peacefully,

01:01:14.490 --> 01:01:16.680
peacefully of old age in their own house,

01:01:16.680 --> 01:01:17.880
I'm like, you know what?

01:01:17.880 --> 01:01:19.860
Death is never a nice thing,

01:01:19.860 --> 01:01:21.920
but if there was a way
that I would want to go

01:01:21.920 --> 01:01:23.100
it would be like that, you know.

01:01:23.100 --> 01:01:25.533
Just nice and peaceful in
the comfort of my own home.

01:01:25.533 --> 01:01:27.150
- Just like very quietly, you know,

01:01:27.150 --> 01:01:28.560
just like reflecting
on that person's life.

01:01:28.560 --> 01:01:30.000
Meanwhile, it's like, oh cool.

01:01:30.000 --> 01:01:31.440
This guy jumped in front of a train.

01:01:31.440 --> 01:01:34.200
I have to pick up his
fucking pieces of his skull.

01:01:34.200 --> 01:01:37.080
You know, I would much
rather do the house job

01:01:37.080 --> 01:01:38.236
than the other one.

01:01:38.236 --> 01:01:39.069
- I know.

01:01:39.069 --> 01:01:39.902
- I know.

01:01:39.902 --> 01:01:44.490
I remember I stayed at an Airbnb once

01:01:44.490 --> 01:01:46.290
and the guy running this Airbnb

01:01:46.290 --> 01:01:48.720
was this old Japanese guy.

01:01:48.720 --> 01:01:51.720
He was in his seventies or something.

01:01:51.720 --> 01:01:52.980
And he was showing us, you know,

01:01:52.980 --> 01:01:54.750
giving us a tour around the house.

01:01:54.750 --> 01:01:57.690
And it's a bit morbid actually,

01:01:57.690 --> 01:02:02.690
but just one block away from
the house was a graveyard.

01:02:03.900 --> 01:02:04.800
Right?
- Oh yeah.

01:02:05.843 --> 01:02:08.700
- And we had to walk
through this graveyard

01:02:08.700 --> 01:02:10.050
to get to his house, right.

01:02:10.050 --> 01:02:15.000
And he pauses at one of the graves

01:02:15.000 --> 01:02:16.230
and he talks about, oh, this is,

01:02:16.230 --> 01:02:20.912
you know, my dad's here,
my granddad is here

01:02:20.912 --> 01:02:22.593
and I'm gonna be right there.

01:02:23.849 --> 01:02:25.323
And me and Sydney go.

01:02:26.610 --> 01:02:27.570
- He's always prepared. He's prepared.

01:02:27.570 --> 01:02:28.403
- Cool.

01:02:29.519 --> 01:02:31.080
How do you react to that?

01:02:31.080 --> 01:02:33.430
How'd you react to, I got
an old guy being like,

01:02:33.430 --> 01:02:34.980
yeah, I'm gonna be there in a few years.

01:02:34.980 --> 01:02:36.736
I'm like-
- Why wait, let's do it now.

01:02:36.736 --> 01:02:39.390
(all laugh)

01:02:39.390 --> 01:02:40.620
Hoe came outta nowhere.

01:02:40.620 --> 01:02:45.090
- But I mean, I can't
even begin to empathize

01:02:45.090 --> 01:02:46.770
what my thoughts would
be like at that age.

01:02:46.770 --> 01:02:49.860
At what point do you just
accept your own mortality-

01:02:49.860 --> 01:02:52.020
- [Joey] I think that's kind of badass.

01:02:52.020 --> 01:02:53.868
- Come to terms with it,
come to peace with it.

01:02:53.868 --> 01:02:54.930
- I think it's kind of badass though,

01:02:54.930 --> 01:02:58.050
to be like, just kind of facing reality

01:02:58.050 --> 01:03:00.420
and being like, you know
what, I'm gonna be there soon.

01:03:00.420 --> 01:03:03.150
I'm just gonna enjoy life,
while I still have it,

01:03:03.150 --> 01:03:03.983
kind of thing, you know?

01:03:03.983 --> 01:03:06.418
- Yeah.
- He's pre-ordering.

01:03:06.418 --> 01:03:07.740
(Garnt laughs)

01:03:07.740 --> 01:03:09.152
He pre-ordered his gravestone.

01:03:09.152 --> 01:03:10.278
- He pre-ordered the gravestone.

01:03:10.278 --> 01:03:11.940
- Pre-ordered death.

01:03:11.940 --> 01:03:13.513
- He had the space, and everything.

01:03:13.513 --> 01:03:15.570
I was just like, "Oh fuck."

01:03:15.570 --> 01:03:16.770
To me, as a young guy,

01:03:16.770 --> 01:03:18.210
to me as a guy in his thirties,

01:03:18.210 --> 01:03:20.880
I was like, "Dude, that's
a bit morbid to me."

01:03:20.880 --> 01:03:22.430
But I mean, I don't know,

01:03:22.430 --> 01:03:24.270
at that age, I dunno how I'd feel.

01:03:24.270 --> 01:03:26.700
