01:50:30.030 --> 01:50:31.560
right, as they're running out of the room.

01:50:31.560 --> 01:50:33.570
- Well it's either that
or it's like an old-

01:50:33.570 --> 01:50:35.610
- Indistinct Asian shouting.

01:50:35.610 --> 01:50:37.257
- It's almost like an old Kung Fu movie.

01:50:37.257 --> 01:50:39.630
And you get into the bad guys' lair.

01:50:39.630 --> 01:50:40.463
- Yeah.
- What's he doing?

01:50:40.463 --> 01:50:42.090
He's sitting down playing Mahjong.

01:50:42.090 --> 01:50:45.061
With his fellow, with his guys
asserting dominance, right.

01:50:45.061 --> 01:50:47.880
That's the image I have of Mahjong.

01:50:47.880 --> 01:50:51.930
Like in that dark smokey room
and everything like that.

01:50:51.930 --> 01:50:54.600
- Well, I feel like
it's the poker of Japan

01:50:54.600 --> 01:50:55.980
or like poker of Asia really?

01:50:55.980 --> 01:50:56.813
- Yeah, definitely.

01:50:56.813 --> 01:50:59.370
- It's like the kind of game
where gambling's not legal,

01:50:59.370 --> 01:51:01.590
but me and the boys can
have a little wager or two,

01:51:01.590 --> 01:51:02.423
you know.

01:51:02.423 --> 01:51:03.944
- As long as it's under
the table, you know?

01:51:03.944 --> 01:51:06.177
(both laugh)

01:51:06.177 --> 01:51:07.560
- 'Cause like, yeah,

01:51:07.560 --> 01:51:09.870
have you guys ever played bridge before?

01:51:09.870 --> 01:51:11.295
- No, I'm not 90.

01:51:11.295 --> 01:51:13.050
- No, no, exactly.
- I've heard of that game.

01:51:13.050 --> 01:51:13.890
What is that?

01:51:13.890 --> 01:51:15.660
That's a game, which I dunno-

01:51:15.660 --> 01:51:16.599
- Is it a card game?

01:51:16.599 --> 01:51:18.115
- QI them. (imitates siren)

01:51:18.115 --> 01:51:19.920
It would've gone off an hour ago.

01:51:19.920 --> 01:51:21.720
- It is a card game.

01:51:21.720 --> 01:51:23.070
I dunno what the images,

01:51:23.070 --> 01:51:25.320
I dunno if it's famous outside of the UK

01:51:25.320 --> 01:51:26.153
or anything like that.

01:51:26.153 --> 01:51:27.270
- Oh yeah, it's famous in the US.

01:51:27.270 --> 01:51:28.800
And again, for being the thing

01:51:28.800 --> 01:51:29.633
that people play in retirement homes.

01:51:29.633 --> 01:51:30.867
- Old people play it, right?
- Yeah.

01:51:30.867 --> 01:51:31.700
- And I remember,

01:51:31.700 --> 01:51:34.080
I think it was in high
school or something.

01:51:34.080 --> 01:51:38.370
One of our teachers for like this...

01:51:38.370 --> 01:51:40.110
you know you have a free period sometimes,

01:51:40.110 --> 01:51:41.280
and it's the end of term.

01:51:41.280 --> 01:51:42.530
You could do whatever you like.

01:51:42.530 --> 01:51:45.900
So this teacher decided
to teach us bridge,

01:51:45.900 --> 01:51:47.820
just teach us the rules.

01:51:47.820 --> 01:51:51.510
I got addicted at a point where
I'd joined the bridge club.

01:51:51.510 --> 01:51:53.580
Yes, I was that kid

01:51:53.580 --> 01:51:54.660
who actually joined the bridge club.

01:51:54.660 --> 01:51:58.290
'Cause it had this image of, like Mahjong,

01:51:58.290 --> 01:52:01.740
being what old retired people
play in retirement homes.

01:52:01.740 --> 01:52:03.720
But like, it is fun as fuck.

01:52:03.720 --> 01:52:04.590
Right?
- Yeah.

01:52:04.590 --> 01:52:07.230
- It's kind of similar to Mahjong

01:52:07.230 --> 01:52:10.110
in a sense where you
get given a bunch of...

01:52:10.110 --> 01:52:13.950
it is kinda like poker
where it is luck based,

01:52:13.950 --> 01:52:15.630
but it's also skill based.

01:52:15.630 --> 01:52:18.120
But what I found most
interesting to bridge

01:52:18.120 --> 01:52:20.820
is that it's a team game. Right?

01:52:20.820 --> 01:52:24.660
So the basic concept is before each game

01:52:24.660 --> 01:52:27.480
you have a partner who sits opposite you.

01:52:27.480 --> 01:52:30.690
And you basically have to make a bet

01:52:30.690 --> 01:52:35.100
on how many points you'd be able to get

01:52:35.100 --> 01:52:36.300
in the hand you're about to play.

01:52:36.300 --> 01:52:39.450
So you're kind of
communicating with your partner

01:52:39.450 --> 01:52:44.450
and communicating and trying
to assess what everyone has,

01:52:45.480 --> 01:52:47.460
by the bets that they're placing.

01:52:47.460 --> 01:52:50.280
And I thought that was like,
I think that's super genius.

01:52:50.280 --> 01:52:53.190
It's basically like UNO, but
before the UNO game starts,

01:52:53.190 --> 01:52:55.320
you have to try and guess
what everyone else has.

01:52:55.320 --> 01:52:57.020
You're working in a team.
- Cool.

01:52:59.190 --> 01:53:00.360
- To me, I wanted to talk about it,

01:53:00.360 --> 01:53:02.430
'cause bridge has this image

01:53:02.430 --> 01:53:05.190
of being a boring game
that old people play,

01:53:05.190 --> 01:53:06.687
but I think it's interesting as fuck.

01:53:06.687 --> 01:53:09.180
And I haven't played bridge
since I was in school.

01:53:09.180 --> 01:53:12.000
When I was in bridge club, I
got super addicted for a while

01:53:12.000 --> 01:53:14.280
because I think it's a
genuinely fun game's.

01:53:14.280 --> 01:53:15.450
- Yeah, it's the same with Mahjong, right.

01:53:15.450 --> 01:53:17.484
I was like, before I started playing it,

01:53:17.484 --> 01:53:18.902
I was like, "No, I'm
not gonna get into this.

01:53:18.902 --> 01:53:20.887
This is what my grandma was into."

01:53:20.887 --> 01:53:21.870
- [Garnt] Yeah yeah, yeah.

01:53:21.870 --> 01:53:23.070
- But then when you
actually start playing it

01:53:23.070 --> 01:53:25.410
you realize just how like intricate it is,

01:53:25.410 --> 01:53:27.510
and it's not just like
a fucking, you know.

01:53:27.510 --> 01:53:29.348
- Well, I didn't think of
it as an old people thing.

01:53:29.348 --> 01:53:31.080
I think of it as a
really complicated thing.

01:53:31.080 --> 01:53:32.940
- But it's interesting though, how,

01:53:32.940 --> 01:53:34.140
even something like Mahjong,

01:53:34.140 --> 01:53:37.470
which is a very complicated, deep,

01:53:37.470 --> 01:53:42.470
and very mind intensive game
is associated with old people.

01:53:42.480 --> 01:53:43.444
- [Garnt] Yeah. Yeah.

01:53:43.444 --> 01:53:45.120
- If you look at like the
pro scene, for example,

01:53:45.120 --> 01:53:47.313
most people are like dudes
and chicks in their 20's.

01:53:47.313 --> 01:53:48.422
- Well yeah, of course.

01:53:48.422 --> 01:53:51.806
It's like chess in the parks in New York.

01:53:51.806 --> 01:53:54.067
It's like, yeah, all the
old dudes are playing it

01:53:54.067 --> 01:53:56.250
but it's the young
people who are the best.

01:53:56.250 --> 01:53:57.570
- It's a PR thing, right?

01:53:57.570 --> 01:53:59.760
When are we gonna get a Twitch category?

01:53:59.760 --> 01:54:02.686
- Everything's a branding
issue, isn't it, guys?

01:54:02.686 --> 01:54:04.650
Nothing can't be fixed with
a bit of branding and PR.

01:54:04.650 --> 01:54:07.140
- Remember, literally just two years ago

01:54:07.140 --> 01:54:08.490
where chess had the same thing

01:54:08.490 --> 01:54:12.060
and then suddenly chess had
a fucking second Renaissance

01:54:12.060 --> 01:54:13.950
with the explosion it's had on Twitch.

01:54:13.950 --> 01:54:16.087
And now it's seen as like
this cool thing that-

01:54:16.087 --> 01:54:17.910
- Mahjong's next.
- Mahjong's next.

01:54:17.910 --> 01:54:19.440
- I'm gonna call it, Mahjong is next.

01:54:19.440 --> 01:54:20.850
- You know what I wanna learn?

01:54:20.850 --> 01:54:22.410
- The Vtubers are already on that shit.

01:54:22.410 --> 01:54:23.460
- Yeah. Yeah.

01:54:23.460 --> 01:54:24.293
That's the secret.

01:54:24.293 --> 01:54:26.400
We need to get the Vtubers
to all start playing it.

01:54:26.400 --> 01:54:29.400
- The Japanese Vtubers have
been on that shit for so long.

01:54:29.400 --> 01:54:30.540
- Yeah. Right.
- Yeah.

01:54:30.540 --> 01:54:33.660
- I think, weirdly chess
is one of those sports

01:54:33.660 --> 01:54:36.180
where when you look at it on paper,

01:54:36.180 --> 01:54:37.950
it just shouldn't be a spectator sport.

01:54:37.950 --> 01:54:38.783
- Mm.
- Yeah.

01:54:38.783 --> 01:54:42.330
- Everything about it makes it
almost a bad spectator sport.

01:54:42.330 --> 01:54:44.700
There's long gaps in between moves.

01:54:44.700 --> 01:54:47.550
It's not that engaging.

01:54:47.550 --> 01:54:51.960
Storylines are a little
harder to get across,

01:54:51.960 --> 01:54:53.760
and it's visually not that exciting.

01:54:54.630 --> 01:54:56.910
You look at a board for
like 15 minutes at a time.

01:54:56.910 --> 01:54:58.940
If you're lucky, they'll
make a move every 15 minutes,

01:54:58.940 --> 01:55:00.300
in the World Championship.

01:55:00.300 --> 01:55:03.180
But somehow I think just out of pure,

01:55:03.180 --> 01:55:06.030
like being a very solid
and ubiquitous game,

01:55:06.030 --> 01:55:08.220
it's managed to somehow carve a good niche

01:55:08.220 --> 01:55:09.660
and a pretty strong one.

01:55:09.660 --> 01:55:12.120
But also chess has also had great success

01:55:12.120 --> 01:55:15.330
by having the branding of
being the smart mans skill.

01:55:15.330 --> 01:55:17.070
The smartest of smarts play chess.

01:55:17.070 --> 01:55:19.590
- Well, I think the problem with chess

01:55:19.590 --> 01:55:23.664
as a spectator sport is
that, with a lot of sports,

01:55:23.664 --> 01:55:26.610
you look at the higher level players,

01:55:26.610 --> 01:55:29.430
you look at, you know,
traditional sports like football,

01:55:29.430 --> 01:55:31.920
basketball, maybe even
like league or whatever.

01:55:31.920 --> 01:55:35.850
And you can, even as a normal person,

01:55:35.850 --> 01:55:37.920
you can appreciate the skill being showed.

01:55:37.920 --> 01:55:40.980
You see someone doing a speed
run of Mario 64 or something.

01:55:40.980 --> 01:55:42.420
You're like, damn those are some-

01:55:42.420 --> 01:55:43.253
- No words needed.

01:55:43.253 --> 01:55:45.660
- Yeah, no words needed. That's
fucking speaks for itself.

01:55:45.660 --> 01:55:49.376
But me watching a high level chess game,

01:55:49.376 --> 01:55:51.550
the chessboard championships is like,

01:55:51.550 --> 01:55:53.102
I've never understood the feeling

01:55:53.102 --> 01:55:55.543
of that side character in "Dragon Ball Z",

01:55:55.543 --> 01:55:57.159
where the characters-
- That explains the moves.

01:55:57.159 --> 01:55:59.846
- Yeah, yeah, no, like
where the main characters

01:55:59.846 --> 01:56:01.409
are following the fight perfectly.

01:56:01.409 --> 01:56:04.242
and you just see, like
you just see nothing.

01:56:04.242 --> 01:56:06.600
This is me watching high level chess.

01:56:06.600 --> 01:56:08.010
- That's why I think chess
has gotten really good

01:56:08.010 --> 01:56:11.430
in recent years at having the,

01:56:11.430 --> 01:56:13.290
kind of what a lot of e-sports are doing

01:56:13.290 --> 01:56:14.999
and what traditional sports
are doing for a long time

01:56:14.999 --> 01:56:16.673
where one of them is the one
that's saying what's going on,

01:56:16.673 --> 01:56:19.410
and one of them's helping add color

01:56:19.410 --> 01:56:21.420
and break it down and really explain it.

01:56:21.420 --> 01:56:22.253
Chess has been really
good at that recently.

01:56:22.253 --> 01:56:25.380
'Cause I mean, I like watching
the World Championships,

01:56:25.380 --> 01:56:27.360
but even then there are periods

01:56:27.360 --> 01:56:29.235
where nothing happens for 20 minutes

01:56:29.235 --> 01:56:34.110
and it's like, I got shit
to do, man, like, come on.

01:56:34.110 --> 01:56:38.460
- Yeah, sometimes I get recommended a clip

01:56:38.460 --> 01:56:40.197
of Magnus Carlsen streaming
or something like that.

01:56:40.197 --> 01:56:42.240
And it's just like an out of context clip

01:56:42.240 --> 01:56:44.820
of this fucking 500 IQ move.

01:56:44.820 --> 01:56:45.653
And then the move happens

01:56:45.653 --> 01:56:47.220
and I don't even know it's happened.

01:56:47.220 --> 01:56:50.190
Because it was like five moves
ago that he reacted to it.

01:56:50.190 --> 01:56:52.680
And I remember,

01:56:52.680 --> 01:56:54.960
I think one of the funniest
videos I've seen is just like,

01:56:54.960 --> 01:56:56.460
I can't remember the exact video,

01:56:56.460 --> 01:57:01.460
but it's a parody video
of someone commentating

01:57:01.560 --> 01:57:03.840
on a Magnus Carlsen stream.

01:57:03.840 --> 01:57:07.650
And it's just a normal person
commenting on his stream.

01:57:07.650 --> 01:57:08.483
And it's just like,

01:57:08.483 --> 01:57:10.620
"And Magnus Carlsen
leaves his knight hanging.

01:57:10.620 --> 01:57:11.453
Is he gonna take it?

01:57:11.453 --> 01:57:12.900
Is the opponent gonna take it?

01:57:12.900 --> 01:57:14.520
No, he doesn't take it.

01:57:14.520 --> 01:57:16.440
He doesn't take the free knight.

01:57:16.440 --> 01:57:18.090
Oh, now he's left his queen hanging.

01:57:18.090 --> 01:57:19.620
Is he gonna take the queen?

01:57:19.620 --> 01:57:21.060
Magnus, the queen is right there.

01:57:21.060 --> 01:57:22.800
No, he doesn't take the queen.

01:57:22.800 --> 01:57:25.860
And he looks like he's in
an awful position right now.

01:57:25.860 --> 01:57:28.140
And he resigns, Magnus Carlsen resigns.

01:57:28.140 --> 01:57:29.187
I don't know what's going on.

01:57:29.187 --> 01:57:30.170
And it's just...

01:57:31.904 --> 01:57:34.675
It's exactly how I feel watching,

01:57:34.675 --> 01:57:36.720
a high level chess game
when I'm just like,

01:57:36.720 --> 01:57:38.160
there is the hanging queen right there.

01:57:38.160 --> 01:57:40.260
Why is Magnus Carlsen not
taking the hanging? Oh.

01:57:40.260 --> 01:57:42.360
And he's just resigned for no reason.

01:57:42.360 --> 01:57:43.680
And he scrolls to the comments section

01:57:43.680 --> 01:57:45.390
and it's never the top comment

01:57:45.390 --> 01:57:46.590
that's explaining what's happening.

01:57:46.590 --> 01:57:48.660
It's always buried a few comments down.

01:57:48.660 --> 01:57:51.060
Just be like, can someone
just explain to me

01:57:51.060 --> 01:57:52.863
what I just witnessed, please.

01:57:53.820 --> 01:57:55.560
But yeah, I mean like that's why,

01:57:55.560 --> 01:57:58.757
one game I really want to get into,

01:57:58.757 --> 01:58:00.450
'cause it sounds super interest,

01:58:00.450 --> 01:58:02.610
it always looks super interesting to me,

01:58:02.610 --> 01:58:06.150
but I'm fucking terrified of
possibly getting into, is go.

01:58:06.150 --> 01:58:09.090
Go seems to me like, that is just like...

01:58:09.090 --> 01:58:10.680
chess is complicated by itself.

01:58:10.680 --> 01:58:15.450
Go, to me, even just the concept of go,

01:58:15.450 --> 01:58:19.560
it seems like what
viewing a bottomless pit

01:58:19.560 --> 01:58:21.123
that you cannot see the end of.

01:58:23.010 --> 01:58:25.770
- My granddad was into go
and he was really good at it.

01:58:25.770 --> 01:58:27.570
And he tried to teach me
when I was in high school

01:58:27.570 --> 01:58:30.720
and I was like, "I don't know
what the fuck is happening".

01:58:30.720 --> 01:58:34.830
I know you're showing me the
ropes really nicely and kindly.

01:58:34.830 --> 01:58:39.830
But all I'm thinking is,
"Ooh, black and white stones".

01:58:40.050 --> 01:58:42.030
- All I know is that Google's documentary

01:58:42.030 --> 01:58:43.980
about the AI they built
to beat the best player.

01:58:43.980 --> 01:58:45.750
- And then I read, "Hikaru No Go"

01:58:45.750 --> 01:58:48.060
and I was like, "I
understand what's happening."

01:58:48.060 --> 01:58:50.910
- No, I like, because I also
read "Hikaru No Go" as well.

01:58:50.910 --> 01:58:51.743
I read all of it.

01:58:51.743 --> 01:58:56.070
And I remember there was a point
where they tried to explain

01:58:56.070 --> 01:58:57.390
what was going on to the viewer.

01:58:57.390 --> 01:59:00.150
And I think halfway through
the manga, they just stopped.

01:59:00.150 --> 01:59:02.400
They just stopped showing the matches,

01:59:02.400 --> 01:59:03.307
and they just like,

01:59:03.307 --> 01:59:06.210
"I'm not even gonna try and
explain it. This guy wins."

01:59:06.210 --> 01:59:10.230
And you see...it's a manga that just,

01:59:10.230 --> 01:59:12.480
it bases its hype on of people,

01:59:12.480 --> 01:59:14.760
off of like character reactions,

01:59:14.760 --> 01:59:16.620
as opposed to what's
going on in the match.

01:59:16.620 --> 01:59:17.543
- It's not very good, is it?

01:59:17.543 --> 01:59:20.642
- I think it's because they
just assume you are like...

01:59:20.642 --> 01:59:21.475
- They figure, you got it.

01:59:21.475 --> 01:59:22.830
- You're 15 volumes in,

01:59:22.830 --> 01:59:24.540
you should know how this works by now.

01:59:24.540 --> 01:59:26.203
We're not gonna explain it to you.

01:59:26.203 --> 01:59:28.950
- I still don't think I
understand how football works

01:59:28.950 --> 01:59:30.880
and I watched all of..

01:59:30.880 --> 01:59:31.713
- What you mean football?

01:59:31.713 --> 01:59:32.546
- Sorry, American football.

01:59:32.546 --> 01:59:33.379
- Oh.
- Oh, okay.

01:59:33.379 --> 01:59:34.440
- Sorry, football.

01:59:34.440 --> 01:59:36.210
I don't know how it football works.

01:59:36.210 --> 01:59:38.400
And I watched, I read
all of "Eyeshield 21".

01:59:38.400 --> 01:59:40.040
I think I understand how it works.

01:59:40.040 --> 01:59:42.420
- I don't think that's very realistic.

01:59:42.420 --> 01:59:43.410
You're not gonna be like,

01:59:43.410 --> 01:59:44.887
you're not gonna be watching
the Super Bowl and be like,

01:59:44.887 --> 01:59:46.740
"Well, he could have done
a devil bat ghost there.

01:59:46.740 --> 01:59:47.573
Why didn't he?"

01:59:47.573 --> 01:59:48.810
- But you know, when
you're watching something,

01:59:48.810 --> 01:59:50.790
you're like, "I feel
like I should understand

01:59:50.790 --> 01:59:52.950
how this base game works yet I don't".

01:59:52.950 --> 01:59:54.840
Baseball's another one that
I've watched stuff about

01:59:54.840 --> 01:59:57.270
and I'm like, "I think I
understand the baseball,

01:59:57.270 --> 01:59:58.560
but I'm not so sure."

01:59:58.560 --> 02:00:02.430
- Really? Baseball's
fairly easy to figure out.

02:00:02.430 --> 02:00:05.220
- I don't understand
when they swap that much,

02:00:05.220 --> 02:00:06.053
but then again, I've never watched-

02:00:06.053 --> 02:00:07.080
- After three strikes, right?

02:00:07.080 --> 02:00:07.980
- Three strikes, right?

02:00:07.980 --> 02:00:09.000
- And they swap teams.

02:00:09.000 --> 02:00:09.870
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

02:00:09.870 --> 02:00:11.550
- So you get three goes
and then everyone...

02:00:11.550 --> 02:00:13.879
No, 'cause then they all,
everyone on the field swaps?

02:00:13.879 --> 02:00:15.451
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

02:00:15.451 --> 02:00:16.284
- Oh.

02:00:16.284 --> 02:00:18.646
- [Crew Member] Better strike out.

02:00:18.646 --> 02:00:21.878
- Yeah yeah, yeah, so, it's
not three throws, right?

02:00:21.878 --> 02:00:23.873
- No, it's three...
- Three strikes.

02:00:23.873 --> 02:00:26.400
So when you throw in baseball,

02:00:26.400 --> 02:00:28.560
